    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "How many solutions do the simultaneous equations $y=2x+3$ and $2x-y=6$ have?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No solutions; the lines are parallel.",
      "worked": "Rearrange the second equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x-y&=6\\\\y&=2x-6.\\end{align*}$$\nThe lines $y=2x+3$ and $y=2x-6$ have the same gradient, $2$, and different $y$-intercepts. They are parallel and never intersect, so there are no solutions.",
